### 11.8 Diagnostics and liveness: the per-writer diagnostic cell

The chapter's two data channels are specified down to their memory ordering. The runtime warning stream (§13.2) and the liveness heartbeat (§12.2) are a third, and they cross between the same tasks.

#### Writers and readers

The diagnostics are written in these places.

- Whichever task stages writes `OutOfClaimEntry`, `ClaimedFaceEntry` and `EntryTypeMismatch` at staging (§11.4). The staging happens on the task of a device (any attached participant in the periphery). It can also go through the harness writer (the framework-owned write path `stage!(sim, …)`, D-200).

- The device tasks write `MalformedDatum`, from the author's loop body via `report!(handle, …)` (§11.6).

- The loop itself writes `DebtReanchor` and `UnboundedRun`.

- The frame (one iteration of the loop) that the loop runs writes `ChatteringBudget` and `FiringBudget` (D-317).

- `attach!` writes `EmptyGreedyClaim` as it adds a device to the roster (the list of attached devices, §11.3). The value goes into the roster entry being created.

`attach!` mutates the roster, so its warning lives in the roster's status rather than on an artifact (D-250). The device's task does not exist until `run!`, so the new roster entry's diagnostic cell (a writer's own holding place for runtime diagnostics) has no other writer yet.

The loop reads the diagnostics. It folds them into the published framework status (the diagnostics value each snapshot carries, §11.2). They hence reach every snapshot (the immutable per-boundary publication). An unspecified structure with those writers is exactly the arbitrary shared mutable state that the two rules of §11.1 exist to eliminate. So the channel gets the mechanism §11.4 already established, not one of its own.

#### One diagnostic cell per writer

Each writer has one diagnostic cell (D-136, D-200). There is one per rostered device, one for the harness writer and one for the loop itself (D-200). The `Model` (the deployment and its executor at one scalar) owns one more. Its frame writes the chattering and firing-budget reports there. The drain (the frame-top swap that applies staged device writes to the root inputs) folds that diagnostic cell into the loop's account beside the loop's own (D-317).

A device's diagnostic cell, the loop's and the `Model`'s each have a single writer, by the same ownership argument as the staging cells (each device's atomic holding place for pending writes). There is no locking, no arbitration and no new primitive (D-136). The harness writer's diagnostic cell is written from whichever task stages, exactly as its staging cell is, and the same CAS (compare-and-swap) append arbitrates (D-200). It carries no heartbeat, and its status record no `task_state`, because it has no task of its own to be alive or dead.

A diagnostic cell holds a bounded accumulation and one atomic liveness timestamp. The accumulation is a small ring of diagnostic values, capacity 16, plus a count per kind (a diagnostic's identity in the closed set) of what the ring could not hold (D-136). The rejected alternatives are in D-136.

#### The bound and the drain

The ring's bound is the rate limit (D-136). When a writer emits past the ring's capacity within one frame, the value is not stored and its kind's suppressed count increments. The drop policy retains the earliest in the frame, and the excess becomes counts. The first occurrences are the ones with diagnostic content, and the hundredth is noise the count already reports. Rate-limiting "wherever its source can repeat" (§13.2) is therefore not a policy layered over the stream but a structural property of the channel that carries it. A chattering model (one exhausting its per-frame localization budget) or a peer flooding malformed datagrams costs at most sixteen retained values and one integer increment per kind per frame, whatever its source does. No writer can starve another, because the diagnostic cells are disjoint.

The diagnostic cells are taken at the same drain §11.4 specifies (D-136). The loop performs one `atomicswap` per diagnostic cell at frame top, at the same point and under the same indivisible-take argument as the staging drain. What the loop swaps in is a shared empty sentinel, so a quiet frame swaps the sentinel in and gets the sentinel back. That allocates nothing, and it leaves no load-only code path that goes untested on healthy runs.

The take also makes publication sound. The binding rule says that nothing reachable from a published snapshot is ever written again (§11.2). Because the taken ring is exclusively the loop's before it is ever reachable from a snapshot, the binding rule holds by construction. The live accumulator is never reachable from a published value.

#### The heartbeat

The heartbeat rides in the same diagnostic cell, as an atomic timestamp field (D-136). The device task stores it on every loop pass from inside the handle primitives. That way the framework observes activity without owning the loop body (§11.6). The loop acquire-loads the heartbeat at publication, when it assembles the status (D-240). There is no separate liveness channel and no second registry. A device that is alive is a device whose diagnostic cell carries a recent timestamp, and the 2 s staleness threshold (§12.2) is read against this field. The heartbeat is not a diagnostic kind. It is a field, always present, never enumerated in Appendix C.

#### The published status

The published framework status is a concrete frozen value (D-136). Per writer it carries four things. `recent` is the ring the frame's drain took, carried by the first publication after that drain, at most sixteen values. `suppressed` is the per-kind counts the ring refused in that drain. `totals` is the cumulative per-writer × per-kind counts since the run began, owned privately by the loop and copied into each status. And `heartbeat` rides beside the `task_state` the loop reads off its own device `Task` handle at publication (§12.2, D-193). Beside the per-writer records ride the pacer diagnostics (§10.7).

Each per-writer record has this shape.

`struct WriterStatus who::String recent::Vector{DiagValue} suppressed::KindCounts totals::KindCounts heartbeat::Union{Nothing,Float64} task_state::Union{Nothing,Symbol} end`

`DiagValue` is any value of the kinds the channel carries, and `KindCounts` is the per-kind counter record that the allocation rules below describe.

Delta plus total is what makes the status legible at any reading cadence. A GUI panel refreshing at 60 Hz sees each occurrence once in `recent`, while a consumer that samples occasionally still reads a complete account from `totals`. Nothing is lost by not looking. The record's `who` is the only attribution a value has, so a renderer presents each value under its record's writer (D-228).

#### Presentation

Presentation is where `maxlog`, the cap Julia's logging macros put on repeats, lives. A status renderer prints a given writer × kind up to 25 cumulative occurrences and then switches to count-only display ("`MalformedDatum` from `UDPInput#3`: 1 482 occurrences"). That threshold is presentation policy, not channel policy (D-136). Counts keep accumulating regardless, nothing recorded depends on it, and the choice belongs to whoever renders. The channel's own bound, above, is the one that is normative.

#### The run's end

The terminal snapshot carries the run's final cumulative counters (§12.4, §13.5, D-136). An unattended run (a run with empty staging and no snapshot readers) that nobody watched therefore still answers "what went wrong, and how often" from the value its own shutdown published. Final means final at the last frame top (D-201). The account closes with the drain that preceded the terminal publication. What lands after it can reach no snapshot. Examples are a report from a device's exit path and the tail's own `DeviceJoinTimeout` (§12.4). So the loop takes each diagnostic cell once more at the run's end, keeping the next run's account clean.

That last take is folded into the termination record (the stopped-sim value naming how the run ended) as the tail residue (§13.5). Per writer, the tail residue is the final ring and its suppressed counts. The take is also presented through the standard logging backend. It is loud and recorded, and still never published (D-201, D-203).

#### Allocation

On a quiet frame nothing allocated scales with diagnostic activity (D-241). The sentinel swap allocates nothing. The per-writer status costs one small vector of records per publication, beside the snapshot allocation per boundary (a published consistency point) that §11.2 already accepts. That is the same GC-over-reuse trade, a roster-sized vector rather than a snapshot type per roster size (D-241).

The per-kind counters are a fixed-shape isbits record, never a `Dict` (D-136). The closed kind set (Appendix C) licenses that, because it makes the counter layout a type rather than a lookup.

On a noisy frame the diagnostic values are allocated at emission, on the writer's own task. A drained non-empty ring is frozen into the snapshot and can never be written again, so the writer allocates a fresh ring lazily at its next emission. That cost, too, lands on the writer's task, and it is the same GC-over-reuse trade §11.2 makes when it rejects preallocated snapshot buffers. The rate limit is therefore an allocation bound as well. One ring of sixteen values per writer per boundary is the worst case, and everything past it is an integer increment. The zero-allocation invariant, scoped to the stepping loop (§9.8, D-305), is untouched. The diagnostic cells sit on the framework side of that scope with publication and logging.

#### Composition with the log

Because the log (the retained sequence of published snapshots) retains snapshot references (§11.2), `totals` is monotone across logged snapshots. `log_every` decimation (the log's keep-every-kth retention policy) therefore loses which boundary within a skipped stretch an occurrence fell on, never how many occurrences there were.

